## 1. Increase uptake and timeliness of vaccination against influenza, COVID-19 and other relevant respiratory infections

**Why this matters in winter:** Respiratory infections contribute to avoidable primary-care consultations, emergency admissions, staff absence and hospital outbreaks. Vaccination is particularly important for older adults, people with chronic disease, pregnant people, care-home residents and frontline staff.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1.1 Use proactive, targeted vaccination invitations and recall** through GP records, pharmacies, maternity services, care homes and community outreach | Clinical/organisational. Identifies eligible people and reduces missed opportunities | **Low–medium:** interoperable registers, administrative capacity, outreach staff and translated communications. Requires risk-stratified call/recall and clear responsibility for follow-up | Evidence that reminders and recall improve vaccination uptake; effects depend on design and baseline uptake. Peer-reviewed evidence is stronger for reminder systems than for any single delivery model (Jacobson Vann & Szilagyi, 2018) | Usually feasible within **3 years**. Barriers include incomplete registers, mistrust, digital exclusion and fragmented responsibility | Uptake by clinical risk group, age, ethnicity, deprivation, care-home status and staff group; missed-opportunity rate; vaccination-related hospital admissions |
| **1.2 Expand pharmacy, mobile, workplace and care-home vaccination delivery** | Organisational/policy. Moves vaccination closer to people and reduces access barriers | **Medium:** trained vaccinators, cold-chain capacity, payment mechanisms, data submission and clinical governance | Pharmacy and community delivery can increase access, although comparative evidence varies by population and country (Isenor et al., 2016) | Feasible within **3 years**, but dependent on workforce and commissioning arrangements. More difficult in remote rural areas and where pharmacy capacity is limited | Proportion vaccinated outside general practice; time from invitation to vaccination; uptake in deprived and underserved communities |
| **1.3 Provide culturally and linguistically tailored communication, including trusted community messengers** | Organisational/policy. Addresses confidence, misinformation and structural access barriers | **Low–medium:** community partnerships, interpreters, paid outreach and co-designed materials | Communication interventions have mixed but generally positive effects; context and trust are important. Evidence is stronger for multi-component approaches than for leaflets alone (Jarrett et al., 2015) | Feasible within **3 years**. Requires sustained engagement rather than one-off campaigns | Uptake by ethnicity and deprivation; survey measures of confidence; refusal and non-response rates |
| **1.4 Offer staff vaccination through convenient, paid-time, on-site routes** | Organisational. Reduces staff absence and protects patients | **Low–medium:** occupational-health capacity, mobile clinics, protected time and rapid recording | Evidence supports convenient access and active promotion, but the effect on staff absence varies (Ahmed et al., 2018) | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Barriers include shift patterns, agency workforce and vaccine hesitancy | Staff uptake by role and site; respiratory-related absence; outbreak-related closures |

**Evidence classification:** 1.1 and 1.2 = **strong/moderate evidence**; 1.3 and 1.4 = **moderate evidence**.  
**Context sensitivity:** Delivery models must be adapted for rural areas, people experiencing homelessness, undocumented migrants and people with limited digital access.

