## 2. Develop hospital-at-home, virtual wards and early supported discharge

These services should be used selectively for patients who can be safely assessed and treated outside hospital, rather than as a general substitute for inpatient care.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **2.1 Provide hospital-at-home for carefully selected patients with acute medical conditions** | Clinical/organisational. Provides hospital-level assessment, treatment and monitoring in the patient’s home, reducing inpatient exposure and potentially improving experience | **High:** multidisciplinary teams, diagnostics, prescribing, rapid response, transport, 24/7 escalation and reliable data systems | Hospital-at-home can produce similar clinical outcomes for selected patients and may reduce institutional care, but patient selection and service quality are critical (Shepperd et al., 2016) | Expansion feasible within **3–5 years**. Barriers include workforce shortages, housing conditions, carer burden, rural travel and unclear accountability | Admissions avoided; escalation to hospital; mortality; readmission within 7 and 30 days; patient and carer experience; cost per episode |
| **2.2 Use virtual wards with explicit clinical inclusion and exclusion criteria** | Organisational/digital. Enables remote monitoring and rapid clinical review for defined conditions | **Medium–high:** devices where clinically necessary, digital platform, clinical response team, interoperability and technical support | NHS virtual-ward evaluations are expanding, but robust comparative evidence remains limited and heterogeneous. Current evidence should be treated as **promising rather than conclusive** (NHS England, 2023a) | Feasible within **3–5 years** in areas with community workforce; more difficult in digitally excluded or low-connectivity populations | Occupancy and utilisation; avoidable admissions; technical failure; response times; equity of access; adverse events; cost per patient |
| **2.3 Implement early supported discharge after stroke, COPD exacerbation and selected surgery** | Clinical/organisational. Transfers rehabilitation and monitoring to the community while reducing length of stay | **Medium:** therapy, nursing, specialist review, equipment and rapid readmission pathway | Early supported discharge is effective for selected stroke patients and can reduce length of stay; benefits are less consistent across all conditions (Langhorne & Baylan, 2017) | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Barriers include community capacity, unsafe home environments and delayed equipment | Length of stay; discharge destination; functional status; readmission; mortality; patient-reported recovery |
| **2.4 Require a safety net and 24/7 escalation route for all hospital-at-home pathways** | Clinical/organisational. Reduces delay in recognising deterioration | **Medium:** shared care plans, escalation protocols, response teams and urgent transport arrangements | Supported by implementation principles and safety evidence, but the independent effect of safety-netting is not well quantified | Feasible within **3 years** if linked to existing urgent community response | Time from deterioration alert to clinical response; unplanned transfer; serious incidents; family/carer concerns |

**Evidence classification:** 2.1 and 2.3 = **strong evidence for selected patients**; 2.2 = **promising, currently undervalued**; 2.4 = **best-practice implementation requirement with limited standalone trial evidence**.  
**High-context sensitivity:** Hospital-at-home is difficult to implement safely without community staffing, diagnostics, prescribing and transport.

