## 3. Strengthen urgent community response, same-day emergency care and admission avoidance

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **3.1 Provide multidisciplinary urgent community response within two hours for people at risk of hospital admission** | Organisational. Rapid assessment and treatment addresses deterioration in the home or care home | **High:** advanced practitioners, therapists, nurses, diagnostics, prescribing and transport | Evidence for admission reduction is mixed and highly dependent on service design. NHS requirements support the model, but peer-reviewed evidence is less definitive (NHS England, 2023b) | Feasible within **3–5 years**, but workforce intensive. Barriers include insufficient community staffing and poor referral integration | Response time; admissions avoided; repeat contacts; transfer to hospital; outcomes at 7 and 30 days |
| **3.2 Establish same-day emergency care pathways for appropriate medical and surgical presentations** | Organisational. Provides rapid diagnostics, treatment and senior decision-making without overnight admission | **High:** ambulatory space, diagnostics, senior clinicians, pharmacy and extended hours | Observational evidence generally shows reduced admissions for selected conditions, but service models differ considerably. Evidence is **moderate and implementation-sensitive** | Feasible within **3 years** in acute hospitals with diagnostic capacity; limited by crowding and staffing | Same-day discharge rate; time to senior review; time to diagnostics; reattendance; conversion to admission; patient experience |
| **3.3 Introduce direct referral routes from general practice, NHS 111, ambulance services and care homes** | Organisational/digital. Reduces unnecessary emergency-department attendance and duplication | **Medium:** referral protocols, clinical advice line, shared records and directory of services | Evidence for direct referral is mainly service evaluation and case-based; likely benefit is greatest where services are reliable | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Barriers include clinical risk concerns, fragmented information and variable local service availability | Referral volume; referral acceptance; ED attendances; time to assessment; rejected or inappropriate referrals |
| **3.4 Provide enhanced care-home clinical support, including planned review and rapid access to clinicians** | Clinical/organisational. Prevents avoidable deterioration and transfers | **Medium–high:** named clinicians, pharmacist/GP input, diagnostics, training and shared escalation plans | Evidence for enhanced care-home models is promising but heterogeneous; admission reductions are not universal (Hockley et al., 2019) | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Requires care-home engagement and payment arrangements | Emergency transfers per 1,000 residents; advance-care-plan completion; response time; avoidable ambulance conveyance |

**Evidence classification:** 3.1 and 3.3 = **mixed/case-based evidence**; 3.2 = **moderate evidence**; 3.4 = **promising but heterogeneous**.  
**Context sensitivity:** These models fail if they simply shift demand without additional community capacity.

