## 4. Prevent and manage frailty, falls and deterioration

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **4.1 Provide comprehensive geriatric assessment for frail older people in the emergency department, acute assessment unit or community** | Clinical/organisational. Identifies medical, functional, medication, social and cognitive needs and creates a coordinated plan | **Medium–high:** geriatricians, therapists, nurses, pharmacy and social-care input | Comprehensive geriatric assessment improves the likelihood of living at home and may reduce institutionalisation when delivered in appropriate settings (Ellis et al., 2017) | Feasible within **3 years** in acute settings; community implementation requires additional workforce | Proportion receiving CGA; admission rate; length of stay; discharge home; functional status; 30-day readmission |
| **4.2 Implement multifactorial falls assessment and intervention for high-risk individuals** | Clinical/community. Addresses balance, strength, medication, vision, footwear and home hazards | **Medium:** physiotherapy, occupational therapy, medication review and home assessment | Multifactorial interventions are effective in selected high-risk groups, although effects vary (Gillespie et al., 2019) | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Barriers include therapy capacity and poor follow-up | Falls and injurious falls; fractures; ED attendances; completion of intervention components |
| **4.3 Establish rapid response pathways after a fall or “near miss”** | Clinical/organisational. Enables assessment before minor injury or illness progresses to hospital admission | **Medium:** community responders, diagnostics, prescribing and care-home protocols | Evidence is less mature than for falls prevention; mainly observational and service-evaluation evidence | Feasible within **3 years**, where urgent community services exist | Ambulance conveyance after falls; time to assessment; repeat falls; hospital admissions |
| **4.4 Provide structured medication and functional review for frail people, particularly before winter** | Clinical. Reduces adverse drug events, hypotension, delirium and loss of function | **Medium:** pharmacist and geriatric review, shared records and follow-up | Evidence for medication review alone is mixed; benefit is more plausible when integrated with CGA and follow-up (Rankin et al., 2018) | Feasible within **3 years**, but difficult at scale without pharmacist capacity | Potentially inappropriate medicines; medication-related admissions; falls; patient-reported medication burden |

**Evidence classification:** 4.1 and 4.2 = **strong/moderate evidence**; 4.3 = **case-based**; 4.4 = **mixed evidence**.  
**Equity relevance:** High, because frailty and falls disproportionately affect older people living alone, people in deprived areas and care-home residents.

