## 5. Earlier diagnosis and treatment of acute respiratory and cardiovascular deterioration

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5.1 Implement structured self-management and supported action plans for COPD and asthma** | Clinical/patient-level. Helps patients recognise deterioration and initiate appropriate treatment earlier | **Low–medium:** clinician training, education, written/digital plans and review | Supported self-management reduces exacerbations and improves quality of life when action plans include education and rapid access to treatment (Lenferink et al., 2017) | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Barriers include health literacy and inconsistent primary-care delivery | Action-plan coverage; inhaler technique; exacerbations; steroid courses; respiratory admissions |
| **5.2 Expand pulmonary rehabilitation and rehabilitation after exacerbation** | Clinical/organisational. Improves exercise capacity and self-management, potentially reducing future utilisation | **Medium:** physiotherapists, exercise space, transport or home-based alternatives | Pulmonary rehabilitation improves exercise capacity and quality of life; effects on admissions vary by timing and population (McCarthy et al., 2015; Puhan et al., 2016) | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Transport, workforce and adherence are major barriers | Referral and completion; exercise capacity; quality of life; exacerbations; readmissions |
| **5.3 Use validated clinical decision support and point-of-care testing selectively to support antimicrobial and respiratory decisions** | Clinical. Improves diagnostic confidence and may reduce inappropriate antibiotics | **Medium:** testing equipment, quality assurance, training and clinical governance | Point-of-care CRP testing can reduce antibiotic prescribing for respiratory infections in primary care, although effects depend on implementation (Aabenhus et al., 2014; updated evidence remains supportive but heterogeneous) | Feasible within **3 years**. Barriers include false reassurance, test quality and poor integration into pathways | Antibiotic prescribing; safety-netting; reconsultation; respiratory admissions; test turnaround |
| **5.4 Create rapid-access pathways for suspected heart failure and cardiovascular deterioration** | Organisational/clinical. Speeds diagnostic echocardiography, specialist review and initiation of treatment | **Medium–high:** diagnostics, specialist nurses, echocardiography and primary-care referral | Evidence supports early diagnosis and guideline-directed heart-failure treatment, but the effect of rapid-access pathway design on winter admissions is less directly established | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Diagnostics and specialist workforce are limiting factors | Time from referral to diagnosis; treatment initiation; heart-failure admissions; mortality; readmissions |

**Evidence classification:** 5.1 = **strong evidence**; 5.2 = **strong evidence for functional outcomes, mixed for admissions**; 5.3 = **moderate evidence**; 5.4 = **moderate indirect evidence**.  
**Promising but undervalued:** Action plans and pulmonary rehabilitation often receive less investment than acute hospital capacity despite evidence for self-management and functional improvement.

