## 6. Improve discharge planning, admission avoidance and integration with social care

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **6.1 Start discharge planning at admission, including estimated discharge date and post-discharge needs** | Organisational. Reduces avoidable delays and coordinates clinical, functional and social-care requirements | **Low–medium:** multidisciplinary huddles, discharge coordinators and shared documentation | Discharge planning interventions can modestly reduce length of stay and readmission, although effects are not always large (Gonçalves-Bradley et al., 2022) | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Requires consistent senior ownership and social-care participation | Length of stay; medically fit days; delayed transfers; readmissions; discharge before midday |
| **6.2 Use “discharge to assess” with rapid review in the person’s usual place of residence** | Organisational/system. Avoids unnecessary inpatient assessment of long-term care and support needs | **Medium–high:** community therapists, social workers, reablement and equipment | UK evaluations are supportive, but peer-reviewed evidence is mainly observational and model-dependent | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Social-care capacity and housing are decisive barriers | Proportion discharged to assess; days saved; care-package timeliness; readmission; patient independence |
| **6.3 Create a single multidisciplinary discharge coordination function across hospital, community and social care** | Organisational/system. Reduces repeated assessments and information loss | **Medium:** coordinators, shared care plans and data-sharing agreements | Evidence is mainly implementation and observational; coordination alone is insufficient without service capacity | Feasible within **3 years**. Governance and information-sharing barriers are substantial | Number of handoffs; failed discharges; delayed-care days; time from referral to package |
| **6.4 Provide seven-day pharmacy, therapy and social-care discharge support for high-risk patients** | Organisational. Prevents weekend delays and post-discharge medication and functional problems | **Medium–high:** weekend staffing and community follow-up | Evidence for seven-day hospital services is mixed; targeted provision is more defensible than universal expansion (Freeman et al., 2016) | Feasible within **3–5 years**, subject to workforce availability | Weekend discharges; medication discrepancies; readmission within 7 days; time to therapy/care package |

**Evidence classification:** 6.1 = **moderate evidence**; 6.2–6.4 = **mixed/case-based evidence**.  
**Highly context-sensitive:** This priority cannot be delivered by hospitals alone and is vulnerable to social-care funding and workforce constraints.

