## 7. Optimise medicines and antimicrobial use

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **7.1 Conduct pharmacist-led medication reconciliation within 24–48 hours of admission and before discharge** | Clinical/organisational. Identifies omissions, duplications, interactions and inaccurate medicines lists | **Medium:** pharmacists, pharmacy technicians, access to primary-care and dispensing records | Medication reconciliation reduces discrepancies, although effects on readmission are variable (Mekonnen et al., 2016) | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Workforce and incomplete records are barriers | Reconciliation completed; discrepancies; medication-related incidents; readmissions |
| **7.2 Target structured medication review and deprescribing for frail people and those with polypharmacy** | Clinical. Reduces adverse effects, falls, delirium and treatment burden | **Medium:** pharmacist/GP review, patient decision support and follow-up | Deprescribing is feasible and can reduce medication burden, but evidence for hospital outcomes remains mixed (Thio et al., 2018) | Feasible within **3 years**. Requires shared decision-making and clinical confidence | Number of medicines; high-risk medicines; falls; adverse drug events; patient-reported burden |
| **7.3 Implement antimicrobial stewardship using audit-and-feedback, prescribing review and delayed prescriptions where clinically appropriate** | Clinical/organisational. Reduces unnecessary antibiotic exposure and resistance without compromising safety | **Low–medium:** stewardship lead, prescribing dashboards, microbiology and training | Audit and feedback, accountable justification and clinician education improve prescribing; effects vary by setting (Davey et al., 2017) | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Risk of over-restriction and diagnostic uncertainty must be managed | Antibiotic prescribing rate and appropriateness; broad-spectrum use; C. difficile; resistance; reconsultation |
| **7.4 Embed renal-dose, anticoagulant and high-risk-medicine alerts into prescribing systems** | Clinical/digital. Prevents predictable medication-related harm during acute illness | **Medium:** electronic prescribing, decision-support governance and alert optimisation | Digital decision support can reduce prescribing errors, but alert fatigue and variable clinical impact are recognised limitations (van der Sijs et al., 2016) | Feasible within **3 years** where electronic prescribing exists; difficult in digitally fragmented systems | High-risk prescribing errors; alert override rate; adverse drug events; renal-dose compliance |

**Evidence classification:** 7.1 and 7.3 = **moderate-to-strong evidence**; 7.2 = **mixed evidence**; 7.4 = **moderate evidence for process outcomes, less certain for clinical outcomes**.  
**Global relevance:** Antimicrobial stewardship and medication reconciliation are highly transferable, including to resource-constrained settings when adapted to local medicines systems.

