## 8. Strengthen infection prevention and control in hospitals, care homes and community services

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **8.1 Use multimodal hand-hygiene programmes** combining infrastructure, training, observation, feedback and leadership | Clinical/organisational. Reduces transmission of respiratory and healthcare-associated pathogens | **Low–medium:** facilities, supplies, education, audit and feedback | Multimodal hand-hygiene strategies are supported by substantial evidence, although measurement of direct patient benefit is imperfect (Allegranzi et al., 2017) | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Sustaining compliance is more difficult than initial implementation | Hand-hygiene compliance; product availability; healthcare-associated infection rates; staff knowledge |
| **8.2 Improve ventilation, air-quality monitoring and isolation/cohorting for respiratory pathogens** | Organisational/policy. Reduces airborne transmission and outbreak risk | **Medium–high:** estates investment, monitoring, maintenance and infection-control procedures | Evidence supports the role of ventilation in reducing airborne transmission, but exact NHS cost-effectiveness varies by estate and pathogen (Morawska et al., 2020) | Low-cost monitoring can begin within **3 years**; major estates changes may take **5–10 years** | CO₂/air-exchange measures; outbreaks; staff sickness; room occupancy and isolation delays |
| **8.3 Introduce rapid testing linked to an operational infection-control response** | Clinical/organisational. Enables cohorting, treatment and isolation decisions | **Medium:** test platforms, laboratory quality assurance, staffing and clear action protocols | Rapid testing can improve diagnosis and infection-control decisions, but outcome benefits depend on turnaround time and response capacity (Brendish et al., 2017) | Feasible within **3 years**. Testing without isolation capacity may have limited benefit | Test turnaround; time to isolation/cohorting; outbreak size; inappropriate antibiotic use |
| **8.4 Provide paid sick leave, occupational-health advice and non-punitive reporting for infectious staff illness** | Organisational/policy. Reduces presenteeism and transmission | **Medium:** occupational-health capacity and workforce cover | Evidence is more indirect and policy-based than trial-based; likely important for workforce safety but difficult to isolate causally | Feasible within **3 years**, but requires workforce planning | Staff working while symptomatic; respiratory absence; outbreaks linked to staff; occupational-health access |

**Evidence classification:** 8.1 = **strong evidence**; 8.2 and 8.3 = **moderate/emerging evidence**; 8.4 = **promising, under-evaluated**.  
**Context sensitivity:** Estates interventions may be difficult for older buildings and financially constrained systems.

