## 9. Expand mental-health crisis alternatives and integrated community support

Mental-health pressures are not limited to winter, but crisis presentations and pressure on emergency departments can increase during periods of reduced community capacity.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **9.1 Provide crisis resolution and home-treatment teams with clear referral criteria and 24/7 senior clinical support** | Clinical/organisational. Treats people in the community and reduces inpatient admission where safe | **High:** multidisciplinary mental-health teams, crisis houses, prescribing and safeguarding | Crisis resolution/home treatment can reduce admission for some patients, but evidence is heterogeneous and implementation quality is crucial (Murphy et al., 2015) | Feasible within **3–5 years** where workforce exists. Safety, staffing and geography are major barriers | Admissions; bed-days; crisis response time; compulsory admissions; patient experience; adverse events |
| **9.2 Establish psychologically informed crisis cafés, safe havens or peer-supported alternatives** | Organisational/community. Offers a non-ED crisis option and earlier support | **Low–medium:** trained peer workers, safeguarding, clinical links and premises | Evidence is mainly observational and service evaluation. These services may improve experience but should not be assumed to reduce admissions without local evaluation | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Not suitable as a replacement for emergency clinical assessment | Attendance; ED diversion; repeat crisis contacts; user-reported safety and usefulness |
| **9.3 Integrate mental-health liaison teams into emergency departments and acute hospitals** | Organisational. Speeds assessment, risk management and onward referral | **Medium–high:** 24/7 liaison staffing, private assessment space and community follow-up | Liaison psychiatry is supported by service evaluations and quality standards; effects on total ED demand are less certain | Feasible within **3–5 years**. Workforce availability and privacy constraints are important | Time to mental-health assessment; ED length of stay; admission rate; follow-up within 7 days |
| **9.4 Provide rapid follow-up after psychiatric crisis or discharge** | Clinical/organisational. Reduces relapse and repeat crisis presentation during a high-risk period | **Medium:** named clinician, care plan, contact within 48–72 hours and crisis access | Early follow-up is widely recommended, but comparative evidence for the optimal timing and model is mixed | Feasible within **1–3 years**. Requires reliable contact details and community capacity | Follow-up within 72 hours; repeat crisis contact; suicide attempts; patient-reported continuity |

**Evidence classification:** 9.1 = **moderate/mixed evidence**; 9.2 = **case-based**; 9.3 and 9.4 = **moderate evidence for process and selected outcomes**.  
**Important caution:** Crisis cafés and peer alternatives are not substitutes for appropriately staffed clinical crisis services.

