## 10. Improve workforce safety, retention and digitally enabled coordination

This priority is an enabling priority: it affects nearly all winter-pressure interventions.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence and classification | Feasibility, barriers and timeframe | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **10.1 Implement evidence-informed staff wellbeing programmes with protected time, team-based interventions and access to psychological support** | Organisational/policy. Reduces burnout and may improve retention, safety and patient experience | **Medium:** protected time, trained managers, occupational health and workload redesign | Individual resilience training alone has limited impact; organisational interventions addressing workload and team functioning are more promising (West et al., 2016; Panagioti et al., 2017) | Feasible within **1–3 years**, but sustained impact requires workforce and job redesign | Burnout; sickness absence; turnover; vacancies; safety incidents; staff engagement |
| **10.2 Use safe staffing escalation plans linked to acuity, absence and demand** | Organisational/policy. Matches staffing to risk and prevents unsafe overcrowding and excessive workload | **High:** staffing reserves, bank/agency arrangements, real-time data and executive escalation | Evidence supports associations between staffing levels, workload and patient outcomes, but the effect of individual staffing tools varies (Griffiths et al., 2018) | Feasible within **3–5 years**; constrained by national workforce shortages | Nurse-to-patient ratios or workload measures; missed care; falls; medication errors; staff injury |
| **10.3 Provide integrated digital care records and shared care plans across urgent, primary, community and social-care services** | Organisational/policy/digital. Reduces duplicated assessment and enables safer transitions | **High:** interoperability, information governance, procurement, training and data standards | Evidence that interoperability improves outcomes and efficiency is promising but less mature than the policy enthusiasm; benefits depend on workflow integration (Adler-Milstein & Pfeifer, 2017) | Local interoperability can improve within **3–5 years**; national integration may take **5–10 years** | Record access; duplicate tests; medication discrepancies; referral completion; discharge delays |
| **10.4 Use winter demand dashboards with predictive analytics only where linked to defined operational actions** | Organisational/digital. Anticipates demand and supports staffing, bed, vaccination and community-capacity decisions | **Medium:** data engineering, analytics, governance and operational ownership | Predictive models are increasingly available, but evidence that they improve patient outcomes is limited. They should be classified as **promising and under-evaluated** | Feasible within **1–3 years** for descriptive dashboards; predictive deployment requires **3–5 years** | Forecast accuracy; response to alerts; bed occupancy; ambulance handover; avoidable admissions; model equity |
| **10.5 Improve occupational safety, violence prevention and manual-handling support** | Organisational. Reduces injury and staff absence while improving patient handling | **Medium:** training, environmental design, security, equipment and reporting systems | Evidence supports multifaceted violence-prevention and safe-handling programmes, but local implementation varies (Hassard et al., 2018) | Feasible within **3 years**. Capital requirements may be significant | Staff injuries; violence incidents; lost-time absence; reporting rates; training completion |

**Evidence classification:** 10.1 and 10.2 = **moderate evidence**; 10.3 = **mixed/emerging evidence**; 10.4 = **promising but under-evaluated**; 10.5 = **moderate evidence**.  
**High-context sensitivity:** Digital integration and staffing reform are difficult in systems with fragmented governance, weak connectivity or severe workforce shortages.

